        <p class="form-hint" id="result-note"></p>
        <div class="table-wrapper salary-table-wrapper">
          <table class="table salary-table">
            <caption class="visually-hidden">The same pay in every period</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Period</th><th scope="col">Gross pay</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody id="salary-rows"></tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- tool-end -->

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Common Salaries as Hourly Rates</h2>
      <p>At 40 hours a week and 52 paid weeks (2,080 hours a year), these round annual figures convert as follows. The same numbers work in any currency.</p>
      <div class="table-wrapper">
        <table class="table">
          <caption class="visually-hidden">Annual salary to monthly, weekly and hourly pay at 2,080 hours a year</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Per year</th><th scope="col">Per month</th><th scope="col">Per week</th><th scope="col">Per hour</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>30,000</td><td>2,500.00</td><td>576.92</td><td>14.42</td></tr>
            <tr><td>40,000</td><td>3,333.33</td><td>769.23</td><td>19.23</td></tr>
            <tr><td>50,000</td><td>4,166.67</td><td>961.54</td><td>24.04</td></tr>
            <tr><td>60,000</td><td>5,000.00</td><td>1,153.85</td><td>28.85</td></tr>
            <tr><td>75,000</td><td>6,250.00</td><td>1,442.31</td><td>36.06</td></tr>
            <tr><td>100,000</td><td>8,333.33</td><td>1,923.08</td><td>48.08</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>A quick rule of thumb at full time: an hourly rate is roughly the annual salary in thousands divided by 2. 50,000 a year is about 25 an hour (24.04 exactly), and 20 an hour is about 40,000 a year (41,600 exactly).</p>

      <h2>When to Use Each Setting</h2>
      <ul class="content-list">
        <li><strong>Comparing a job offer with your current pay:</strong> enter both salaries with their real weekly hours. A higher salary with longer hours can be a lower hourly rate.</li>
        <li><strong>Moving from employee to contractor:</strong> enter the day rate you are offered, set days per week, and add unpaid weeks for holidays, sickness and gaps between contracts.</li>
        <li><strong>Part-time work:</strong> enter your part-time hours. A 0.6 full-time role at 24 hours a week on 30,000 a year is still 24.04 an hour, the same as 50,000 at 40 hours.</li>
        <li><strong>Budgeting:</strong> use the monthly figure, which is annual ÷ 12, rather than four weekly payslips.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Employers sometimes use a different divisor for hourly rates. The US federal government uses 2,087 hours, the average number of working hours in a year once leap years are counted, and some UK employers use 52.14 weeks. Adjust weeks per year if your contract states one of these.</p>
    </section>
